WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.780
Take a patient's skin cells, reset them, and grow them for weeks in a spinning

2
00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:10.720
flask. Can that model their early cortex?

3
00:00:11.833 --> 00:00:15.013
First, the skin cell must go back to an embryonic state.

4
00:00:15.953 --> 00:00:17.873
Four transcription factors can do that.

5
00:00:18.783 --> 00:00:23.563
Transcription factors are proteins that bind DNA and switch genes on or off.

6
00:00:24.517 --> 00:00:28.777
The result is an induced pluripotent stem cell, an iPS cell.

7
00:00:29.637 --> 00:00:32.757
Pluripotent means it can become any cell type of the body.

8
00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:38.200
A line made from a patient carries the patient's genome, mutation included.

9
00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:41.160
It can also pick up new changes in culture.

10
00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:43.910
The recipe has four steps.

11
00:00:44.870 --> 00:00:46.830
First, gather the stem cells into a ball.

12
00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:49.340
Steer it toward neural tissue.

13
00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:53.550
Embed it in a droplet of gel.

14
00:00:56.050 --> 00:01:00.490
Then spin it in a flask, where moving medium brings more oxygen and nutrients.

15
00:01:01.583 --> 00:01:04.223
Nothing tells these cells where they are in a body.

16
00:01:05.043 --> 00:01:06.463
Which features appear on their own?

17
00:01:12.833 --> 00:01:16.253
Zones of progenitors form around a fluid-filled cavity.

18
00:01:17.313 --> 00:01:18.873
Young neurons settle outside them.

19
00:01:19.783 --> 00:01:21.203
No one placed these cells.

20
00:01:22.063 --> 00:01:23.063
They arranged themselves.

21
00:01:23.963 --> 00:01:27.563
That is self-organization, the idea behind every organoid.

22
00:01:31.367 --> 00:01:34.587
But the six layers of mature cortex form at best partly.

23
00:01:35.407 --> 00:01:37.787
And a standard organoid has no blood vessels.

24
00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:41.130
Now set a fetal cortex beside it.

25
00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:45.370
Some of what builds it comes from the cells, some from the body around them.

26
00:01:46.333 --> 00:01:48.033
Blood vessels bring oxygen.

27
00:01:49.053 --> 00:01:51.893
Microglia, the brain's immune cells, arrive from outside.

28
00:01:53.013 --> 00:01:54.773
Axons bring input from the thalamus.

29
00:01:55.733 --> 00:01:59.733
And interneurons stream in from another region, the ventral forebrain.

30
00:02:00.717 --> 00:02:03.317
In a single organoid, most of these are missing.

31
00:02:04.417 --> 00:02:06.557
Here is the one idea to carry through.

32
00:02:07.437 --> 00:02:08.617
The cells bring the program.

33
00:02:09.457 --> 00:02:10.957
The embryo brings the surroundings.

34
00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:15.640
An organoid keeps the program and loses most of the surroundings.

35
00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:19.480
So trust it most for what the cells do on their own.

36
00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:23.580
Test it hardest wherever development needs something from outside.

37
00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:27.880
And measure every feature, because the dish adds stress of its own.
